<?xml version="1.0" standalone="yes"?>

<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "godwit">
<!ENTITY author    "kmbrimble">
<!ENTITY version   "0.2.1">
<!ENTITY launch    "Settings/Godwit">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kmbrimble/unraid-godwit/main/godwit.plg">
<!ENTITY gitURL    "https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-godwit/releases/download/v&version;">
<!ENTITY plgPATH   "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY emhttp    "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY md5       "1c10a68bfaf6386d2bd64c28739e9223">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;" author="&author;" version="&version;" launch="&launch;" pluginURL="&pluginURL;" support="https://github.com/kmbrimble/unraid-godwit" min="7.0" icon="upload">

<CHANGES>
###0.2.1
- Found live on the host during 0.2.0's own verification (not caught by
  code review, which doesn't run `ps aux`): rcd's generated `--rc-user`/
  `--rc-pass` were on the spawned process's command line, visible to any
  other local process via `ps aux` or its /proc/PID/cmdline. Fixed by
  passing them as `RCLONE_RC_USER`/`RCLONE_RC_PASS` environment variables
  instead — confirmed locally that the bundled rclone v1.75.1 honours
  these identically to the flags. No functional change otherwise.
###0.2.0
- Phase 2: a Remotes section on the Godwit page lists configured rclone
  remotes (name, type, health status, quota) and can add a Google Drive or
  OneDrive remote from a pasted `rclone authorize` token, re-authorise,
  test-connect and delete one — all via rcd's config API, never the bundled
  CLI, so a live rcd always sees the change immediately. No client_secret,
  client_id or token value is ever returned to the browser. godwitd now
  health-checks every configured remote hourly (read-only `operations/about`
  only — no writes to Drive/OneDrive in this phase), stores the result in
  SQLite, detects auth-expiry distinctly from other errors, and sends an
  Unraid notification once per OK/failed transition and once per 90% quota
  crossing.
- Uninstall now preserves &plgPATH; (rclone.conf and any future
  godwit.cfg/jobs.json) instead of deleting it — it holds live OAuth
  credentials as of this release. Only the installed .txz package files are
  removed from that directory.
###0.1.3
- The install block bundled a new .txz on every upgrade into
  /boot/config/plugins/godwit/ but never removed the old one (~20MB each,
  rclone bundled in) — confirmed on the live host holding both the 0.1.1
  and 0.1.2 package files. After upgradepkg succeeds, the install block
  now deletes every old godwit-version.txz in that directory except the
  one just installed, matching only the numeric-version .txz shape so it
  never touches rclone.conf or any other file there. Safe under set -e
  when the glob matches nothing, and no files are deleted if upgradepkg
  itself fails.
###0.1.2
- Fixes a boot-time install failure: the install block runs under `set -e`,
  and array-ready.sh's expected non-zero exit at boot (array not started,
  cache not mounted — the exact scenario the 0.1.1 deferral branch exists
  for) was left as a bare statement, so `set -e` aborted the whole install
  block before it ever reached the deferral branch, reporting the plugin
  install as failed. The result is now captured via an `if`/`else` instead
  of a bare command.
###0.1.1
- Fixes a boot-order bug: plugins install ~14s before the cache pool is
  mounted, so 0.1.0's unconditional `rc.godwit start` in the install step
  could create godwit's state dir on the RAM rootfs, silently covered by
  the later cache mount and lost at shutdown. godwitd now starts via the
  `started`/`stopping_svcs` emhttpd event hooks (matching file.activity,
  unbalanced and tips.and.tweaks), and the install step only starts it
  immediately when the array is already up. godwitd also refuses to run at
  all if `/mnt/cache` isn't a real mountpoint, and `rc.godwit stop`
  escalates to SIGKILL (daemon and its rcd child) if a graceful stop times
  out.
###0.1.0
- Initial scaffold release: plugin installs, registers an empty Settings/Godwit
  page, and runs a supervised daemon (rc.godwit / godwitd) that starts and
  restarts a single bundled rclone rcd process (unix socket, or a
  127.0.0.1 fallback with generated credentials — never --rc-no-auth). No
  remotes, jobs or uploads yet — this release proves the packaging and
  release pipeline plus D10's bundled, SHA256-verified rclone binary.
</CHANGES>

<FILE Name="&plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz">
<URL>&gitURL;/&name;-&version;.txz</URL>
<MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<!--
Install step. Every script invoked below is checked with `-f` (file exists),
never `-x` (executable bit) — an executable-bit check can silently skip a
script that lost its bit in transit. See CLAUDE.md non-negotiable 4.
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
set -e

# Stop any already-running daemon from a previous version first — rc.godwit's
# start is idempotent (a no-op while a live pid exists), so without this an
# upgrade would silently leave the OLD process (and its rcd child) running
# under the newly unpacked files instead of restarting it.
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit ]]; then
  bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit stop || true
fi

# Remove any previously installed package version before upgrading — leaves
# no stale package entry in /var/log/packages from an older release.
for pkg in /var/log/packages/&name;-*; do
  if [[ -f "$pkg" ]]; then
    removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")"
  fi
done

upgradepkg --install-new &plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz

# Delete superseded package files from flash — only after upgradepkg has
# succeeded. Each .txz bundles rclone (~20MB); leaving every past version
# on the USB flash drive on every upgrade is wasteful and unbounded.
for old in &plgPATH;/&name;-[0-9]*.txz; do
  if [[ -f "$old" ]]; then
    if [[ "$old" != "&plgPATH;/&name;-&version;.txz" ]]; then
      rm -f "$old"
    fi
  fi
done

mkdir -p &plgPATH;

# Plugins install ~14s before the cache pool mounts at boot (confirmed from
# /boot/logs/syslog on the live host) — starting godwitd unconditionally
# here, as 0.1.0 did, can create its state dir on the still-unmounted
# /mnt/cache's RAM rootfs. Only start now if the array is already up (a
# mid-session install/upgrade); a boot-time install defers to the
# `started` event hook, which fires once the array and cache are actually
# ready. See array-ready.sh.
# The install block runs under `set -e`, so array-ready.sh's expected
# non-zero exit (the whole point of this check, on the exact boot-time
# scenario this release exists to handle) must be consumed by an `if`
# condition, never left as a bare statement — a bare statement's failure
# would abort the install script before it ever reaches the "defer to the
# started hook" branch below.
READY=1
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/array-ready.sh ]]; then
  if bash &emhttp;/scripts/array-ready.sh; then
    READY=0
  else
    READY=1
  fi
fi
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit ]]; then
  if [[ "$READY" -eq 0 ]]; then
    bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit start
  else
    echo "&name;: array not started yet — godwitd will start via the 'started' event hook"
  fi
fi

echo "&name;: install complete"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!--
Remove step. Stops the daemon (which stops its rcd child), removes the
package and the installed tree, and any runtime pid/log leftovers. As of
0.2.0, &plgPATH; (rclone.conf and any future godwit.cfg/jobs.json) is
deliberately preserved — it now holds live OAuth credentials for Google
Drive/OneDrive remotes, and losing them on every uninstall/reinstall cycle
would mean re-authorising by hand each time. Only the installed .txz package
files are removed from that directory; everything else there survives. See
README.md for how to delete rclone.conf by hand if a full wipe is wanted.
-->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
if [[ -f &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit ]]; then
  bash &emhttp;/scripts/rc.godwit stop || true
fi

removepkg &name;-&version; || true

rm -rf &emhttp;
rm -f &plgPATH;/&name;-*.txz
rm -rf /var/run/godwit

rm -f /var/run/godwit.pid
rm -f /var/log/godwit.log

echo "&name;: removed (rclone.conf and any other config preserved under &plgPATH;)"
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
